<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">American Revolutionary War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1775">1775</a>–<a href="/years/1783">1783</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as American War of Independence · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The American Revolutionary War was fought between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and its rebel colonies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, which declared independence as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1776. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> joined against Britain, and German states such as Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a> and Ansbach hired out soldiers to the British. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1783">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1783 recognised American independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war.jpg" alt="American Revolutionary War" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Trumbull · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Surrender_of_Lord_Cornwallis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the British victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>, which ended with the Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> of 1763, tensions grew between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and its Thirteen Colonies. The war's debt and demands from British taxpayers for cuts in spending meant that Parliament expected the colonies to pay for their own defence. The Sugar Act and Stamp Act brought new taxes, and the Rockingham ministry repealed the Stamp Act in 1765 but asserted Parliament's authority in the Declaratory Act. After the Boston Massacre of 5 March 1770 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/intolerable-acts">Intolerable Acts</a> of 1774, twelve colonies sent delegates to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-continental-congress">First Continental Congress</a>, which passed the Continental Association in October 1774, a boycott of British goods.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The war began on 19 April 1775 with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battles-of-lexington-and-concord">Battles of Lexington and Concord</a>, after which British troops lost around 300 men on their withdrawal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>. In June 1775 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-continental-congress">Second Continental Congress</a> created the Continental Army and unanimously appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> its commander. It adopted the Declaration of Independence on 4 July 1776, the text written almost exclusively by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a>. The British captured New York City in November 1776, but Washington crossed the Delaware on the night of 25 to 26 December and overwhelmed the Hessians at Trenton, taking 900 prisoners.</p><p>In October 1777 a British force under John Burgoyne surrendered at Saratoga, which convinced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> that American independence was viable. France signed a Treaty of Alliance in February 1778, and Spain allied with France against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1779. The British then turned to the southern colonies, where they had wrongly assumed strong Loyalist support. In 1779 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sullivan-expedition">Sullivan Expedition</a> ravaged the lands of the Haudenosaunee allied with Britain. A Franco-American army besieged General Cornwallis at Yorktown, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, in September and October 1781, and the French navy cut off his escape, so that he surrendered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Fighting largely ceased in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> after Yorktown, though the British wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> went on for two more years. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1783">Treaty of Paris</a>, signed on 3 September 1783, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> recognised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> and independence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. The Treaties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> settled its conflicts with France and Spain, and Britain gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobago">Tobago</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> and small territories in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to France, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menorca">Menorca</a>, West Florida and East Florida to Spain. Historian Terry M. Mays describes the war as three kinds of conflict: a colonial dispute over trade regulation as well as taxation, a civil war between Patriots, Loyalists and the neutral, and a wider war with France and Spain.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “American Revolutionary War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Spain recaptured most of West Florida in the <b>American Revolutionary War</b>, and the Peace of Paris of 1783 returned both Floridas to it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-florida">Spanish Florida · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1783 the last two of the three treaties of the Peace of Paris, ending the <b>American Revolutionary War</b>, were signed at the palace.</blockquote><a href="/work/palace-of-versailles">Palace of Versailles · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>American Revolutionary War</b> was going badly for the Continental Army, which had been defeated in New York and had retreated through New Jersey.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>Meeting at the Maryland State House in Annapolis, the Confederation Congress proclaimed its ratification of the Treaty of Paris, the official end of the <b>American Revolutionary War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-14">14 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It happened during the <b>American Revolutionary War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-8">8 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>With four to five hundred volunteers and Natives and little support from Massachusetts, Eddy had tried to bring the <b>American Revolutionary War</b> to Nova Scotia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-29">29 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
